1
00:00:05,479 --> 00:00:07,560
Hello, I'm Sangsoo Jeong.

2
00:00:07,561 --> 00:00:13,320
This time, let's do the sketch, the first stage of concept art practice.

3
00:00:13,321 --> 00:00:18,200
First, here's the planning document we briefly made last time.

4
00:00:18,201 --> 00:00:28,120
To quickly go over it: gender is female, age is twenty to thirtyish, class is magic, era is future fantasy, and the value and hue is a bright blue tone.

5
00:00:28,121 --> 00:00:33,480
And for what to concentrate on, I'll use a cape and coat to give the silhouette.

6
00:00:33,481 --> 00:00:39,960
The keyword, drawn out from a clean and simple design, was polygons, transparency, and light.

7
00:00:41,445 --> 00:00:46,160
And I mentioned that I'd borrow from the healer concept I drew.

8
00:00:47,045 --> 00:00:49,120
And I gathered a few references.

9
00:00:49,310 --> 00:00:54,480
I mainly want to use this material where polygon shapes fold.

10
00:00:54,481 --> 00:00:56,560
And I'd like the light to come out like this too.

11
00:00:56,640 --> 00:00:58,480
I'm not sure if it'll be expressed well.

12
00:00:58,481 --> 00:01:02,041
Once you've appreciated the references a bit, you get into the work.

13
00:01:03,205 --> 00:01:06,480
Without any stiff explanation, let's get right to it.

14
00:01:06,560 --> 00:01:09,600
Naturally, you have to draw the human body first.

15
00:01:09,601 --> 00:01:11,920
I draw the human body carefully.

16
00:01:18,464 --> 00:01:23,020
I don't tidy up well, and my surroundings are always messy.

17
00:01:23,021 --> 00:01:25,340
You can see that in the picture too.

18
00:01:25,341 --> 00:01:27,580
I can't draw clean lines either.

19
00:01:27,581 --> 00:01:32,860
And I have a quick temper, so I often put color before the lines are even organized.

20
00:01:33,020 --> 00:01:36,540
For a sketch, I do tend to only do the parts that are really needed.

21
00:01:36,541 --> 00:01:42,661
Still, since it's a demo picture, let me draw it a little more in detail than usual and start.

22
00:01:50,429 --> 00:01:57,440
When drawing the human body, I said you should draw thinking about the slope of the shoulders and pelvis.

23
00:01:57,441 --> 00:01:58,441
Let me check.

24
00:02:03,044 --> 00:02:06,560
It's working well.

25
00:02:06,561 --> 00:02:14,000
This picture, like the healer concept I've been showing, I'll draw in a pose that's visible to the camera.

26
00:02:14,001 --> 00:02:20,960
Actually, as I mentioned in Chapter 3, I'm not good at working when someone's watching me.

27
00:02:20,961 --> 00:02:25,760
Drawing while recording feels like dozens of people are watching next to me.

28
00:02:25,761 --> 00:02:26,800
Honestly, it's a lot of pressure.

29
00:02:27,310 --> 00:02:30,880
So I decided to demo with a familiar picture I've drawn many times.

30
00:02:31,774 --> 00:02:36,330
And since I can't multitask, I'm recording first and explaining after.

31
00:02:42,780 --> 00:02:50,430
Anyway, a tip when posing is to think of yourself as an actor and actually act out the pose with your body.

32
00:02:50,431 --> 00:03:01,550
For example, if you're drawing a psychopath character, think as if you really are a psychopath, or think of the psychopath acting you see in movies and express it with your body.

33
00:03:01,551 --> 00:03:07,470
What you have to be careful of here is that an overly exaggerated pose is unsuitable for concept art.

34
00:03:07,471 --> 00:03:10,590
You can show character personality through facial expression and pose.

35
00:03:11,010 --> 00:03:16,290
But you must not hide or obscure the design with an exaggerated pose and composition.

36
00:03:18,244 --> 00:03:25,040
The reason I like this pose is, should I say its presence is alive because it stretches out in all directions, up, down, left, right?

37
00:03:25,041 --> 00:03:27,280
That's why it's a composition and pose I often use.

38
00:03:29,445 --> 00:03:33,161
While progressing the human body somewhat, I also envision the clothing along with it.

39
00:03:36,360 --> 00:03:41,800
If you gathered references and appreciated them a lot, inspiration will have come up naturally.

40
00:03:41,801 --> 00:03:45,960
At the very least, you'll have a direction for how to start.

41
00:03:45,961 --> 00:03:48,040
Let me start the design lightly.

42
00:03:49,765 --> 00:03:51,840
I said the silhouette is important.

43
00:03:51,841 --> 00:03:55,520
Design it broadly, focusing on the silhouette.

44
00:03:55,521 --> 00:03:57,760
Don't consider the details.

45
00:03:57,761 --> 00:04:04,521
Silhouettes can be inspired by uniforms or long coats I've always thought looked cool.

46
00:04:04,910 --> 00:04:09,920
Work so it's not excessive, like something that exists in reality, like something out of a movie.

47
00:04:11,190 --> 00:04:15,960
It's good to compose it simply and basically.

48
00:04:15,960 --> 00:04:21,640
I've liked slightly loose cardigans or oversized fits.

49
00:04:21,641 --> 00:04:25,320
You can see that reflected in my drawings too.

50
00:04:25,321 --> 00:04:28,641
So it's good to start from what exists in reality.

51
00:04:30,910 --> 00:04:35,520
For example, let me assume I'm drawing a male character wearing medieval armor.

52
00:04:35,521 --> 00:04:45,920
There'd be differences by era, but the commonly known full plate is a composition of body, arm, leg armor, etc., worn over chainmail.

53
00:04:45,921 --> 00:04:47,680
The composition doesn't deviate from the big framework.

54
00:04:48,400 --> 00:04:55,280
So I design with a basic composition, then modify it.

55
00:04:55,360 --> 00:05:00,480
As a side note, unless the client makes a special request, I don't make multiple options.

56
00:05:01,175 --> 00:05:08,850
I think focusing on one picture and designing it is much better in terms of cost.

57
00:05:08,851 --> 00:05:11,170
If you miss the mark, just do one more.

58
00:05:11,220 --> 00:05:18,130
I don't think you need to draw four, five, even ten options from the start.

59
00:05:18,131 --> 00:05:19,730
Well, that's my opinion.

60
00:05:19,731 --> 00:05:21,410
But if your boss says to do it, you have to.

61
00:05:33,505 --> 00:05:37,420
Let me also flip left and right, and shrink the size.

62
00:05:37,421 --> 00:05:40,220
You'll see some part that looks off.

63
00:05:40,495 --> 00:05:43,781
The leg looks weird, so I'm erasing and redrawing it.

64
00:05:44,805 --> 00:05:49,600
If you think some part looks off, the chances it really is off are high.

65
00:05:49,601 --> 00:05:55,920
If you think, eh, this is probably fine, the chances that it's not fine are high.

66
00:05:55,921 --> 00:05:57,520
Really amazing, right?

67
00:05:57,521 --> 00:05:59,920
I've always done that.

68
00:05:59,921 --> 00:06:04,960
Doing that, I get obsessive too, and there were times I'd spend the whole day just on the face.

69
00:06:06,360 --> 00:06:08,410
But honestly, no answer comes out.

70
00:06:09,260 --> 00:06:15,690
The best method is to look at it the next day, the day after, and the day after that.

71
00:06:15,691 --> 00:06:19,690
As I draw, I take breaks, play games, and go on the internet.

72
00:06:21,020 --> 00:06:25,950
Is that a freelancer's privilege?

73
00:06:25,951 --> 00:06:32,110
At least after a few tens of minutes, or a few hours, when you look again, you'll see the parts you missed.

74
00:06:32,111 --> 00:06:39,710
Doing that, sometimes you do a major overhaul, and it eats up a lot of time too, but the picture gets that much better.

75
00:06:39,711 --> 00:06:44,350
I tend to think, even when drawing one picture, let me do it properly.

76
00:06:44,351 --> 00:06:48,750
Rather than several pictures that are fifty or seventy percent of my ability.

77
00:06:49,100 --> 00:06:56,710
I think drawing one picture that's a hundred or a hundred and ten percent of my ability helps my value more.

78
00:06:57,850 --> 00:07:02,300
Once the silhouette is somewhat established, let me roughly set the details too.

79
00:07:03,260 --> 00:07:11,580
Rather than focusing on line precision, it's important to work big, with the feeling of envisioning the upcoming work.

80
00:07:11,581 --> 00:07:20,140
New inspiration felt from simple and rough lines can combine with the inspiration I already have, producing yet another inspiration.

81
00:07:21,790 --> 00:07:28,880
When drawing a pattern like this, it's important not to break the flow and volume of the clothing.

82
00:07:28,881 --> 00:07:32,521
It can look too simple and flat.

83
00:07:33,715 --> 00:07:38,300
In the current case, I need to bring out the feeling of curling into the body.

84
00:07:53,125 --> 00:07:55,120
I'll draw the details.

85
00:07:56,085 --> 00:07:59,641
These polygon elements will be applied throughout.

86
00:08:05,685 --> 00:08:09,390
What's important when designing is continuity.

87
00:08:09,391 --> 00:08:13,790
Use the elements you used in one place in another place.

88
00:08:13,791 --> 00:08:14,830
Continuously.

89
00:08:17,240 --> 00:08:24,170
Intentionally clashing different elements to draw the eye is also good, but I don't enjoy it.

90
00:08:24,171 --> 00:08:28,730
So I keep the design keywords to one or two, simply.

91
00:08:34,200 --> 00:08:40,330
I'll concentrate all the design elements on the upper body and the collar part, which is a collection of triangles.

92
00:08:40,331 --> 00:08:43,571
I'll draw the rest only enough to support the upper body.

93
00:08:53,400 --> 00:08:56,490
I keep drawing the pattern.

94
00:08:57,935 --> 00:09:01,610
I have to draw considering the clothing folds; it's hard.

95
00:09:01,611 --> 00:09:03,930
I think I'll suffer a lot while coloring.

96
00:09:06,200 --> 00:09:10,170
While coloring, I'll need to build up the volume and redraw the pattern.

97
00:09:21,779 --> 00:09:22,780
So I'll just draw it moderately.

98
00:09:22,781 --> 00:09:25,580
I keep drawing while tidying up.

99
00:10:05,985 --> 00:10:09,621
Once it's somewhat progressed, I'll lay down the base color.

100
00:10:10,565 --> 00:10:13,840
I'm not going to go into coloring like this.

101
00:10:13,841 --> 00:10:22,761
With color laid behind like this rather than a white canvas, you can feel the volume more than just lines, so awkward parts are easier to spot.

102
00:10:24,700 --> 00:10:27,870
I've put up a duplicate view to see the whole thing.

103
00:10:31,605 --> 00:10:35,469
I explained how to bring that up back in Chapter 3.

104
00:10:38,195 --> 00:10:41,541
I'll split the layers to make coloring easier.

105
00:10:42,250 --> 00:10:46,780
The more a part is layered on top in the picture, the higher its layer should be.

106
00:11:30,899 --> 00:11:31,900
I'll also apply the color I envisioned.

107
00:11:31,901 --> 00:11:33,500
I'll tidy up the lines again.

108
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:40,960
Create one layer under the sketch layer, apply white, then adjust the opacity so the lines are visible.

109
00:11:42,970 --> 00:11:46,380
I gradually firm up the design.

110
00:11:46,381 --> 00:11:49,740
It's not much different from the working process of coloring.

111
00:11:49,741 --> 00:11:52,860
You'll keep seeing this view from now on.

112
00:11:52,861 --> 00:11:57,660
It'll be a repeated process of drawing lines, painting, drawing lines, painting.

113
00:12:33,660 --> 00:12:37,861
I've spent about thirty to forty minutes on this sketch.

114
00:12:38,910 --> 00:12:49,920
Usually I draw for hours, but thinking what if it's boring to watch, how if the video gets too long, I drew a bit hurriedly.

115
00:12:49,921 --> 00:12:55,881
I recommend that you sketch with more time, pondering and envisioning deeply.

116
00:12:58,225 --> 00:13:02,650
Next chapter, we'll work hard to color this picture.

117
00:13:03,535 --> 00:13:04,535
Thank you.
